        <p class="profile-detail-region">Language · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Tamil</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 300 BCE – present</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Family</span><span class="info-stat-val">Dravidian, southern branch; descended from Proto-Dravidian, most likely spoken around the third millennium BCE. Its closest major relative is Malayalam, which began to diverge around the 9th century CE.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Writing</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-brahmi">Tamil-Brahmi</a> in its earliest inscriptions; later vaṭṭeḻuttu, alongside Grantha and Pallava, until the present Tamil script: 12 vowels, 18 consonants and the āytam, 247 characters in all, with six Grantha letters for borrowed sounds.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Earliest records</span><span class="info-stat-val">Short inscriptions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-brahmi">Tamil-Brahmi</a>, attested since c. 300 BCE; earlier dates are claimed for writing on burial urns at Adichanallur (from at least 696 BCE) and a Keezhadi sample (around 580 BCE). The earliest long text is the Tolkāppiyam, whose oldest layers may date to the late 2nd century BCE.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Status today</span><span class="info-stat-val">Living: spoken by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamils">Tamil people</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, Puducherry and northern and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and by communities worldwide; official in Tamil Nadu and Puducherry, an official language of Sri Lanka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, and in 2004 the first recognised classical language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</span></div>
