    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Tamil is a Dravidian language attested since about 300 BCE in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-brahmi">Tamil-Brahmi</a> inscriptions, and one of the longest-surviving classical languages in the world.</li><li>Its early literature includes the Tolkāppiyam, the oldest Tamil grammar, and the 2,381 Sangam poems, dated between the 1st century BCE and the 5th century CE; Tamil inscriptions have been found as far away as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</li><li>Old Tamil became Middle Tamil by the 8th century and Modern Tamil from 1600; today Tamil is official in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, Puducherry, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, and in 2004 it became the first recognised classical language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Scholars trace the English word <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> to Tamil arici, through loans from the Tamil of 400 BCE into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> and then into Greek óruza.</p></aside>
    </section>

